WEBVTT

00:00:01.270 --> 00:00:13.950
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Chana Joffe-Walt, sitting in for Ira Glass. So a couple of weeks ago, I was calling principals and teachers to find out what it was like to be in schools.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这是《美国生活》。我是Chana Joffe-Walt，代替Ira Glass主持。几周前，我给校长和老师们打电话，想了解学校里的现状。

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:18.270
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>I called Principal Brian Cox in Wyoming. Did he have a minute to talk?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我给怀俄明州的Brian Cox校长打了电话。他有时间聊聊吗？

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:22.350
<v Brian Cow>Um, I can, yeah. I've got to be out on bus duty in, like, 15 minutes.
<v Brian Cow>嗯，可以。我大概15分钟后得去负责校车调度。

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:37.590
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Principal Cox normally does not do bus duty. He also does not normally teach French class, because he's a principal, and he doesn't know French. He did both the week we spoke, because 20% of his staff were absent. Omicron.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Cox校长平时不用负责校车。他平时也不教法语课，因为他是校长，而且他不懂法语。但在我们通话的那一周，他两样都干了，因为他手下20%的员工缺勤了。因为奥密克戎。

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:49.440
<v Brian Cow>You know, I told somebody today when we walked in-- we had three paraprofessionals down in very key special education classrooms-- and I told them our goal today is to be a hockey goalie and just keep it out of the net.
<v Brian Cow>你知道，我今天刚跟人说过——我们有三名关键特殊教育教室的助教缺岗——我告诉他们，我们今天的目标就是像冰球守门员一样，别让球进网。

00:00:49.440 --> 00:01:04.560
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Two things I learned from calling around to lots of educators over the last few weeks-- school is still a mess. That's the first thing. And number two, the situation in schools right now can only be described using metaphor.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>过去几周里，我给很多教育工作者打过电话，学到了两件事——学校依然是一团糟。这是第一点。第二点，目前学校的情况只能用比喻来描述。

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:14.070
<v Brian Cow>We're just going to play for eight hours today and try to keep as few of them from getting by us today. And then we'll recoup tonight and figure out how we can recover for tomorrow.
<v Brian Cow>我们今天只是要坚持八个小时，尽量少让学生溜走。然后晚上休整一下，想想明天怎么补救。

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:20.820
<v Amy Gallaway>Oh, man. This year-- [CHUCKLES] I'll try not to cry. This year has been hard. [CHUCKLES]
<v Amy Gallaway>天哪。今年——（轻笑）我尽量不哭。今年太难了。（轻笑）

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:28.740
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Amy Gallaway, a teacher in Alaska, says, I thought this year was finally going to be normal. This year is not.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>阿拉斯加州的老师Amy Gallaway说，我本以为今年终于能恢复正常了。但并没有。

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:33.240
<v Amy Gallaway>Like a creaky, creaky bridge that we're trying to get across.
<v Amy Gallaway>就像我们正努力穿过一座吱吱作响的桥。

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:37.170
<v Math Teacher>The kind of step where you're not going to die, you're going to roll your ankle.
<v Math Teacher>那种不会丧命，但会崴到脚的步伐。

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:39.470
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>A math teacher in New York City.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>一位纽约市的数学老师说道。

00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:51.040
<v Math Teacher>And land with your face in a mud puddle and be like, OK, I could have probably navigated that obstacle a little bit differently.
<v Math Teacher>然后脸朝下摔进泥坑里，心想，好吧，我本可以换种方式绕过那个障碍的。

00:01:51.040 --> 00:02:03.210
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>There are not enough teachers. There are not enough school bus drivers. You never know which students will come in person, if at all. Some kids have just disappeared. Enrollment and attendance-- way down.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>老师不够用。校车司机也不够用。你永远不知道哪些学生会到校，甚至不知道他们会不会来。有些孩子直接消失了。入学率和出勤率——大幅下降。

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:12.150
<v Takeru Nagayoshi>The metaphor that I would always use is like, it's as though you are creating a vehicle--
<v Takeru Nagayoshi>我经常用的比喻是，这就像你在制造一辆车——

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:16.590
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>You can tell this guy is about to go in really deep, right? English teacher.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>听得出来，这位老兄快要深入探讨了，对吧？英语老师。

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:34.820
<v Takeru Nagayoshi>--while driving it at the same time on a road whose conditions are unclear in terrible weather. That vehicle, by the way, is a school bus. And not only are there 120 kids there, you're also driving from the back of the bus.
<v Takeru Nagayoshi>——同时还得在天气恶劣、路况不明的道路上开着它。顺便说一下，那辆车是一辆校车。不仅里面有120个孩子，你还是在后座驾驶。

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:37.020
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>[LAUGHS] That's a metaphor that never ends.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>（笑）这真是一个没完没了的比喻。

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:39.840
<v English Teacher>Yeah. It just compounds.
<v English Teacher>是啊。负担只会越来越重。

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:58.260
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Takeru Nagayoshi, a high school teacher in New Bedford, Massachusetts. Mr. Nagayoshi was Massachusetts teacher of the year for 2020. In 2021, he quit his job-- quit his job, right after being publicly recognized as the very best.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Takeru Nagayoshi，马萨诸塞州新贝德福德的一名高中老师。Nagayoshi先生是2020年马萨诸塞州的年度教师。2021年，他辞职了——就在被公开表彰为最优秀教师之后，他辞职了。

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:17.940
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>He was at the top of his game, and he walked away, which is a pretty huge reversal. He didn't plan to leave public school teaching. When schools closed and everything went online, Mr. Nagayoshi missed the classroom deeply. He missed all the things you can only do when everyone is in a room together.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>他正处于职业生涯的巅峰，却选择离开，这是一个巨大的转折。他没打算离开公立学校的教学岗位。当学校关闭、一切转为线上时，Nagayoshi先生非常怀念教室。他怀念所有只有大家聚在一起才能做的事情。

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:49.320
<v Takeru Nagayoshi>The group activities, the collaborative mood board work that my kids do, writing on anchor charts, breaking up into small groups, doing practice rounds of public speaking, and Socratic seminars, and peer writing, and taking out your pens so that you can highlight things on your papers, and give that feedback, and offer commentary to each other's research questions. And there are so many opportunities and magic that I think happens in person. And when I think about learning, it's such a social thing.
<v Takeru Nagayoshi>小组活动、孩子们做的协作情绪板、在挂图上书写、分组、进行公开演讲练习、苏格拉底研讨会、同伴写作、拿出笔在纸上标出重点、给出反馈、对彼此的研究问题发表评论。我认为当面交流会产生很多机会和魔力。当我思考学习时，这真的是一件社交性的事情。

00:03:49.320 --> 00:04:05.400
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>He was used to feeling inspired every day, energized. But even when they were back in person, Mr. Nagayoshi never got that feeling back. His students didn't get to school on time or regularly at all. And they just weren't focused.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>他习惯了每天感到振奋和充满活力。但即使恢复线下授课后，Nagayoshi先生再也没找回那种感觉。他的学生们没能准时到校，甚至根本不规律。他们根本无法集中注意力。

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:24.150
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>They were grieving for people they loved. They were stressed or depressed, juggling lots of responsibilities, or staying up until 4:00 AM playing video games. And they were just out of the habit of doing school. School had been such a regular routine before, for kids, for everyone.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>他们为挚爱之人的离世而悲伤。他们感到压力或抑郁，应对着繁重的责任，或者熬夜到凌晨4点玩游戏。他们只是失去了上学的习惯。在这之前，对于孩子们，对于所有人来说，学校曾经是如此规律的日常。

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:42.990
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Mr. Nagayoshi told me he started to question if what they were doing was really serving kids. He questioned the most basic things about the way school functions. Like, why did the school day have to be so long? All the rigid routines-- why do they have to learn in class periods? Why every day?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Nagayoshi先生告诉我，他开始怀疑他们所做的一切是否真的服务于孩子。他质疑学校运作方式中最基本的事情。比如，为什么学校的一天必须这么长？所有那些僵化的常规——为什么他们必须在课堂时间内学习？为什么每天都要这样？

00:04:42.990 --> 00:05:02.240
<v Takeru Nagayoshi>Sometimes, I just felt more like a gatekeeper to, like, archaic ways of learning and convening. I don't know, Chana, I just feel like the seal has been broken, and I don't-- and it's hard for me to imagine a world to go back to that magic.
<v Takeru Nagayoshi>有时，我感觉自己更像是过时学习和集会方式的守门人。我不知道，Chana，我只是觉得封印已经被打破了，我——我很难想象一个能回到那种魔力的世界。

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:06.650
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Whoa. What seal has been broken? What does that mean?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>哇。什么封印被打破了？那是什么意思？

00:05:06.650 --> 00:05:25.560
<v Takeru Nagayoshi>The-- [SIGHS] what is the seal that has been broken? I think, our relationship to what school is.
<v Takeru Nagayoshi>那个——（叹气）那个被打破的封印是什么？我想，是我们与学校究竟是什么之间的关系。

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:43.860
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It's been two years since school buildings first closed. It's kind of hard to remember how impossible that seemed before it happened-- closing entire school buildings. School seemed like a fact, inevitable. If you're a kid, you go to school five days a week. It's inescapable.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>距离校舍首次关闭已经过去两年了。很难回想起在发生之前，关闭整个校舍看起来是多么不可能的事情。学校似乎是一个事实，不可避免。如果你是个孩子，你每周就要上五天学。这是逃不掉的。

00:05:43.860 --> 00:06:05.190
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>But then, it wasn't anymore. Buildings closed for months, more than a year. This happened in lots of different ways across the country. It was worse for some, better for others. But talking with teachers and parents and kids, it's clear that school as they left it March 2020 does not look the same as the school they have now.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>但后来，不再是这样了。校舍关闭了数月，甚至超过一年。这种情况在全国各地以各种不同的方式发生。对一些人来说更糟，对另一些人来说则好一些。但与老师、家长和孩子们交谈后，很明显，2020年3月他们离开时的学校，和他们现在拥有的学校已经不一样了。

00:06:05.190 --> 00:06:24.180
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Classrooms still close. School buses don't come. Or teachers are out sick, or there aren't enough teachers. Some districts have just decided kids don't need to come in on Fridays now. In the state of Missouri, 1/4 of the school districts have now decided to switch to four days of school a week, instead of five.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>教室依然会关闭。校车不再来。或者老师生病缺勤，又或者老师不够用。一些学区干脆决定学生现在周五不用来学校了。在密苏里州，四分之一的学区现在决定将学校从一周五天上课改为四天上课。

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:45.150
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Some kids stopped coming altogether. School had always been this solid object that now seems much less solid. So many of the people I've been speaking with are questioning the very premise of school, whether it's worth it, whether they need to show up every day, whether they need to be in a classroom.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>有些孩子完全停止了上学。学校曾经是一个坚固的实体，但现在看起来远没有那么稳固了。我采访的许多人都在质疑学校的前提本身，质疑它是否值得，质疑他们是否需要每天出现，质疑他们是否需要待在教室里。

00:06:45.150 --> 00:07:09.810
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It's like everyone involved-- kids, parents, drivers, teachers, superintendents-- all realized at the same time, hey, this treadmill has an off switch. This place is not fact. School is not inevitable. Just listen to the way Mr. Nagayoshi talks about when school was inevitable, back before the pandemic, just a few years ago.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>感觉就像所有相关的人——孩子、家长、司机、老师、督学——同时意识到，嘿，这台跑步机是有开关的。这个地方不是既定事实。学校并非不可避免。只要听听Nagayoshi先生谈论大流行之前，也就是几年前学校还是“不可避免”的时候，他是怎么说的。

00:07:09.810 --> 00:07:19.080
<v Takeru Nagayoshi>Sure, it was hard, and things were never perfect, but it was a different time. And I don't know if we can ever get back to that. I think it's a bygone era.
<v Takeru Nagayoshi>当然，那时候也很难，情况也从不完美，但那是一个不同的时代。我不知道我们是否还能回到过去。我认为那是一个逝去的时代。

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:23.640
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Like, the way we've done public school for more than a century?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>就像我们进行了一个多世纪的公立学校教育方式？

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:24.870
<v Takeru Nagayoshi>Mm-hmm.
<v Takeru Nagayoshi>嗯嗯。

00:07:24.870 --> 00:07:26.340
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>[CHUCKLES] It's gone?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>（轻笑）它消失了？

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:29.260
<v Takeru Nagayoshi>I don't-- I think it is.
<v Takeru Nagayoshi>我不——我想是的。

00:07:29.260 --> 00:07:56.760
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Is it? Is school as we've known it over? Today, I want to bring you the stories of two kids who have drifted away from school. And we see what happens when they try to make their way back. Stay with us.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>是吗？我们所熟知的学校教育结束了吗？今天，我想给你们讲两个从学校生活中“漂走”的孩子的故事。我们来看看当他们试图回归时会发生什么。请继续收听。

00:07:56.760 --> 00:08:16.050
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>The last couple of months, I've been talking with principals, teachers, parents, and kids about school and what's changed for them two years into a pandemic. And I keep returning to that question-- have schools fundamentally changed? One of the people I've been checking in with, on and off for a while now, is a woman named MJ and her daughter, Neeah.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>过去几个月里，我一直在和校长、老师、家长和孩子们谈论学校，以及在大流行两年后，对他们来说发生了什么变化。我不断回到那个问题——学校从根本上改变了吗？我一直在关注的人之一，是一位名叫MJ的女士和她的女儿Neeah。

00:08:16.050 --> 00:08:49.720
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>They're a family of three. MJ is a single parent. Neeah is her youngest, and she has an older daughter, who's grown. They're mixed-race, live in Michigan with a French bulldog. During the pandemic, MJ and Neeah constructed a brand-new and very unusual life with one another. And seeing how much has changed for them, where they ended up after two years of pandemic school, that, maybe more than anyone else I've spoken with, made me feel like, whoa, maybe school as we once knew it truly is over.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>她们是三口之家。MJ是一位单亲妈妈。Neeah是她最小的孩子，她还有一个已经成年的大女儿。她们是混血儿，住在密歇根州，养着一只法国斗牛犬。在大流行期间，MJ和Neeah共同构建了一种全新的、非常不寻常的生活。看到她们的生活发生了多大的变化，看到她们在大流行学校生活的两年后处境如何，这让我感觉，也许比我采访过的任何人都更有理由觉得，哇，也许我们曾经熟知的学校真的结束了。

00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:57.840
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So here's Act One, Big Mother Is Watching. It begins, of course, when the schools closed two years ago.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>所以这是第一幕，“大母神”在看着你。故事开始于两年前学校关闭的时候。

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:02.040
<v MJ>Oh, my god. You want to talk about stress? Man.
<v MJ>哦，天哪。你想谈谈压力吗？天哪。

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:02.730
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>What happened?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>发生了什么？

00:09:02.730 --> 00:09:06.690
<v MJ>Oh, yeah. It just-- like, what do you do?
<v MJ>哦，是的。这简直就是——你该怎么办？

00:09:06.690 --> 00:09:21.750
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>MJ is a building engineer. She had to go to work. She supervises all the maintenance for buildings she works in around Detroit-- make sure the boilers, lighting, carpentry are all attended to. So at first, she just brought Neeah to work.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>MJ是一名建筑工程师。她必须去工作。她负责监督底特律周边她工作区域内所有建筑的维护工作——确保锅炉、照明、木工都得到照看。所以起初，她只是把Neeah带到工作场所。

00:09:21.750 --> 00:09:30.810
<v MJ>It was fun, you know what I mean? She didn't get in my way, running around while I'm cleaning up, you know, that area that I have to. So you know, I rode the wave as long as I could.
<v MJ>这很有趣，你懂我的意思吗？她在我不碍事的时候到处跑，我也在清理我必须清理的区域，你知道的。所以，我尽可能久地维持着这种状态。

00:09:30.810 --> 00:09:40.830
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>When her boss made it clear that this was not a long-term arrangement, MJ dropped Neeah with an aunt, then a friend, a babysitter. A neighbor took her for a week.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>当她的老板明确表示这不是长久之计后，MJ把Neeah托付给一位阿姨，然后是朋友，还有保姆。邻居带了她一周。

00:09:40.830 --> 00:09:57.180
<v MJ>You know, even if it's just a day, OK, exactly how long can they help? You know, throw them money. How much can you afford? Absolutely nothing, but we'll make it work anyway. It's a never-- it's a whole thing. It's exhausting, mentally. Exhausting, OK?
<v MJ>你知道，即使只是一天，好吧，他们到底能帮多久？你知道，给他们点钱。你能负担得起多少？完全负担不起，但我们还是想办法做到了。这从来不是——这是一整套麻烦。这让人精疲力竭，精神上。精疲力竭，懂吗？

00:09:57.180 --> 00:10:24.150
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And it was months. MJ had a new thought. Neeah was nine years old. She could prepare food herself, get herself dressed. A lot of parents leave kids her age at home alone. But MJ hadn't before, certainly not all day. And she did not want to now, but she had to go to work. She'd be 10 minutes away. She could come home at lunch to check in. Neeah would be on Zoom all day, with a teacher from school.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>而且持续了几个月。MJ有了一个新想法。Neeah九岁了。她可以自己准备食物，自己穿衣服。很多家长把这个年纪的孩子单独留在家。但MJ以前没这样做过，肯定没试过整天。她现在也不想这样，但她必须去上班。她离家只有10分钟路程。她可以在午饭时间回家看看。Neeah会整天开着Zoom，跟着学校的老师上网课。

00:10:24.150 --> 00:10:37.140
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>A while back, a friend had recommended security cameras when MJ moved into a new house. MJ ordered a couple. Neeah stayed home alone. MJ left, telling herself, I've got the cameras.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>很久以前，MJ搬进新家时，一位朋友推荐了安防摄像头。MJ订购了几个。Neeah独自留在家中。MJ出门时安慰自己说，我有摄像头看着呢。

00:10:37.140 --> 00:10:57.750
<v MJ>I could see her in different areas of the house. And I have an alarm system that, if any window or doors opened, it alerts the police and makes a really loud noise. And you know, depending on the area of the house that Neeah's in, she knows what the escape route is and what the emergency plan is.
<v MJ>我可以看到她在房子的不同区域。我还有一个警报系统，如果有任何窗户或门被打开，它会报警并发出很大的声音。而且你知道，根据Neeah所在的房子区域，她知道逃生路线和紧急计划是什么。

00:10:57.750 --> 00:11:09.630
<v MJ>You know, it's not something that I wanted to do. I wasn't happy to do it, but I can see my kid. You know, like I said, I got a friend behind me, my neighbor across-- the old lady across the street. Gotta love them, you know?
